  "success_stories": "On March 29, 2025, the Wilton Police Department received a report of public indecency involving a male subject who had allegedly exposed himself to two juvenile victims. The victims provided a description of the suspect's vehicle, which was searched in Flock\u2019s database, yielding a match. Shortly thereafter, a neighboring police department responded to a similar report involving the same suspect vehicle, in which the operator allegedly exposed himself to a juvenile within their jurisdiction. With the objective evidence obtained from Flock the offender was identified and subsequently charged with Risk of Injury to a Child, Breach of Peace in the Second Degree, and Public Indecency.\n\nOn May 15, 2025, the Wilton Police Department received a report of an elderly female resident who had failed to return home. A search of the Flock database revealed that her vehicle had recently been observed in New York State. The individual had no known reason for traveling to that location, and her family determined that the trip likely occurred during an episode of mental confusion. She was formally reported as missing, and local New York law enforcement were advised of the missing individual but could not locate her. The vehicle's registration was added to a Flock hotlist. Several hours later, on May 16, 2025, at approximately 12:25 a.m., Flock notified officers that the vehicle was actively traveling within the Town of Wilton. Responding personnel located the vehicle and successfully reunited the individual with her family.\n\nMultiple area police departments were experiencing a crime pattern of organized retail theft. Through collaborative investigations, law enforcement identified a suspect vehicle and entered its registration into a Flock hotlist. On June 21, 2025, the Wilton Police Department received a Flock alert indicating that the suspect vehicle had entered the town. Officers initiated a search of local shopping centers and located the vehicle parked in the lot of a grocery store. While engaging the vehicle's operator, a second individual emerged from the store pushing a shopping cart with merchandise. Upon observing the officers, the second individual attempted to depart the scene. Further investigation revealed that the operator and the second individual were traveling together. The merchandise in the cart was confirmed to have been shoplifted from the store. Additionally, during the course of the investigation, fentanyl was discovered inside the vehicle. In summary, the Flock Safety alert enabled officers to locate the suspect vehicle, identify its occupants, and interrupt a larceny in progress.\n\nOn July 16, 2025, Flock LPR cameras in the Town of Wilton alerted officers to a vehicle traveling through the area, whose registered owner was the subject of an active arrest warrant for domestic violence. Utilizing the vehicle details provided by Flock, officers located the vehicle and took the subject into custody without incident.\n\nData & Usage Summary",
